A||||4||87GTZ34||poot-poot no more...||||1||
Z||000000||87GTZ34||07-05-2001||04:47 PM||||Back in March someone (I don't recall whom), had a post about an intermittent miss and a couple of people said they also had the same symptom. 'AkursedX' posted on the same topic in mid June as well.  At that time I said that I had the parts to perform some repairs on my car (that the previous owner's butcher had screwed up) that may affect the inconsistent miss.  I put in a new timing set, fixed a few oil leaks, oil pressure sensor and oil pump, yada yada yada.  Anyway my theory was that the 'poot' was caused by the timing slack but the intermittent miss may or may not be another root cause.  Here are my results:<BR>The timing chain was hanging off of the crank gear and the back of it was rubbing the timing cover.  I took b4 and after pics but I won't have time to post (due to a USB reconfig issue on my PC) until I get back from a trip next week.  The timing delta as measured w/ a timing light was just over 10 degrees... in one direction.  That means that if the cam were to 'bounce' it could mean a 20 degree (crank) change in timing.  Well the new chain & gears have cured the 'poot' but I still have a slight unevenness at idle.  I used to feel it 'chug' driving at any 'legal' speed with an even load.  This is not a idle hunting computer BTW but a slight unevenness from one cyl fire to the next and it is not periodic.  Bottom line is that I think my fuel system is causing this unevenness as it hasn't been serviced in 156K  mi except for fuel filters.  That unevenness would cause the cam to 'bounce' meaning that the timing from one cylinder to the next would change for both valve and ignition timing with the main impact being the ignition timing.  If the bounce went from one extreme to the other, it would like changing your ignition timing 20 degrees and back real fast.  The actual 'poot' sound is the pressure drop and recovery of the exhaust system caused by miss-fire.  If anyone wants to check there cam chain slack you can use a rough method with the harmonic balancer and a drinking straw in the #1 cyl.  This will save buying a degree wheel and setting it up.  The procedure:<BR>1) take out #1 spark and insert the straw<BR>2) bring the crank clockwise around to 6 degrees BTC<BR>   this will take out the rod direction change at TDC and only look at the cam chain.<BR>3) have someone watch the straw and even put slight pressure on it with a finger.<BR>4)turn the crank counter-clockwise until the straw moves and measure the degree delta on the harmonic balancer.<P>I am going to service the fuel system next and will update this post when I do.  I do expect the fixes to to even out my engine firing.<P> Hope this helps and good luck.  Email me if you have any specific questions but I probably won't answer for a week if you ask tomorrow.||||reg||1
Z||000001||My7Fieros||07-05-2001||09:54 PM||||Ok that sounds interesting. Like I stated in AdkurstX's other thread, I have a sim prob. Lemme try to explain it here, rather than to start another "intermitten miss" thread:<BR>88 Formula (107 thousand on the clock)<BR>I replaced the plugs, wires, rebuilt the dist (basically dismantled it, cleaned it, changed the ignition controle mod, pick up coil, cap, and rotor, as well as the gasket to stop the perverbial dist. oil leak), oil sending unit, temp sensor (guage didnt work, does now due to replacement), coolant sensor, battery, and alot of other "non mechanical" parts.....<BR>Well back around Christmas it started this thing were it'll (upon start up) itll do the "poot poot", and when it does you can see the tach jump and squirm with each "poot". Its slow to start to, sounding like an old WWI fighter plane starting up.<BR>It <I>seems</I> to do ok while driving for the most part (keep in mind, since it started this, Ive stopped driving it...only to drive it around the apt complex about once a month for a couple of minutes), but at idle its horrible...even stalling at times.<BR>Obviously I dont drive it now because Ive got this <B>major</B> phobia about having it die at a red light, not to restart.<BR>The car was my daily driver for several months with no probs...then just started this the day after Christmas. So, at that time I decided to park it and drive my 86 Se..so the Formula has pretty much sat there w/ the exception of the minor "battery savor" drives around the lot I previously mentioned. I feel confident its getting a good spark do to all the new ignition parts I put on it.....fuel del prob perhaps? (yes I can hear the fuel pump whine at first). Could it be as simple as a new fuel filter? Or something as complex as mentioned above? Please help, I really dont want to have to sell it, one reason being the obvious probs and Ill lose money, not to mention I really love this car (see "Formula restoration complete! Pics! in the search function)...any ideas? Thanx, M7F||||reg||1
Z||000002||87GTZ34||07-06-2001||12:35 AM||||My7, The tach behavior you mentioned was the same as mine was b4 changes.  In my case (so far but it is early), the new chain & gears got rid of the reaction to a miss and my tach no longer jumps.  As I mentioned though, I still have a subtle unevenness to it at idle.  I suspect my injectors as they were in rough shape when I did the valve job in March.  I used the best 'looking' injectors from this car and a set from a previous 2.8 that I had.  Not a good way to match injectors...  I plan to have my injectors serviced/matched & I think that will fix the rest of my problem.  One thing that I saw today when I got it running again confirms my suspicion and that is when it is 1st started, it was smoking (and its only 14) black stuff for a few puffs.  This would indicate that at least 1 injector is probably bleeding down when shut off.  If that is true then it is also leaking while running and when enough fuel accumulates to form a drop and gravity takes over, that creates the mis-fire.  As I mentioned previously, it no longer 'poots' but the miss is noticeable to someone with a good 'engine ear'.  I would suggest the rough cam chain check to see if you have considerable slop.  If you do then slight mis-fires will cause cam bounce and intensify the effect of the mis-fire on the engine's smoothness.||||reg||1
Z||000003||My7Fieros||07-06-2001||07:33 PM||||I'll check that out....I'll let you know the results. Be sure to post an update after you finish yours as well...Im really wanting to fix this thing. Thanx, M7F||||reg||1
Z||000004||87GTZ34||09-04-2001||10:53 AM||||I mentioned previously that I would update here if I had any new findings.  Recently I replaced my injectors and rebuilt the fuel rail (regulator mostly) and changed the O2 sensor in an effort to resolve the intermittant mis-fire and little and no load. The reason I did this is that when I had the heads off for the head rebuild (1000 mi ago), I noticed that the inectors were pretty wet around the caps w/ a lot of carbon buildup. They had 156K on them at that point. I had another set from a car w/ 105K that I sent to Marren Auotsport for cleaning and eval. He could only get three of the 6 in range so I went with new or slightly used. The O2 sensor I replaced because the previous owner had knocked out the unleaded gas opening in the filler neck and the cat was totally burned out. I previously replaced the cat about 800 mi ago. While I was working on it this time I took the plugs out for a plug read and found that in 1000 miles three went bad. Two of them had a wierd crack/hole in the ceramic around the center electrode and in additon had a wierd color.  The third was really strange... the center electrode was pushed over to the side so that is was now mis-aligned with the end electrode.  This plug was not this way when I put it in.  Those physical changes and wierd coloring was due to detonation I think. Also while I had the hood up, I checked everything (with a vacuum hose) with a vacuum check pump/gauge. I had to bypass the EGR solenoid/switch and evaporation canister (until I have funds to buy new ones)as they were defective and causing vacuum leaks. I think the detonation (that damaged the plugs)and the mis-fire was caused by a combination of the bad injectors, the vacuum leaks, no EGR (just replaced the valve when I did the heads too), and the fact that the heads had been shaved (real good) by the previous owner's mechanic.  When I put it all back together, it purred like a 'smooth engine' but I hear it pinging on 89 octane gas.  I'll update this post again when I resolve the pre-ignition.  First step will be to up the octane.||||reg||1
